# AuntieOS admin: local/build environment.
#
# WHAT DOES *NOT* BELONG HERE
# Every real server secret lives in Google Secret Manager and is bound per
# function via `secrets: [...]` in the function's options. That includes
# SMTP2GO_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM, TWILIO_*, STRIPE_*, TRIBE_PIN_PEPPER,
# WEB_API_KEY, AUNTIE_OPERATOR_UIDS, SENTRY_DSN, MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN and
# CLOUDINARY_*. Do not copy any of them into a .env file: a function reads the
# bound secret, not this file, so a value here would be silently ignored while
# looking authoritative.
#
# REMOVED 2026-07-23, do not re-add
# The Baserow block (BASEROW_URL/TOKEN/EMAIL/PASSWORD/JWT/REFRESH/DATABASE_ID
# and the five BASEROW_TABLE_* ids) and N8N_URL/N8N_TOKEN: both services were
# retired and the only readers left were root-level Python scripts that are
# themselves dead. AUNTIE_SERVER_HOST/PORT and AUNTIE_SHARED_SECRET went with
# them: they had zero consumers anywhere in the repo, in any language.

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_MODEL were removed 2026-07-23 along with the 19
# root-level Baserow/n8n Python scripts that were their only readers. Every
# deployed Anthropic path uses Secret Manager: web/functions binds the key, and
# web/functions-python reads its own directory or its binding. Do not re-add
# them here.

# --- Web admin (Vite / React) ---
#
# WHERE THESE COME FROM, and it is no longer this file alone.
# Every VITE_* variable either web app reads is declared in
# scripts/client-secrets.mjs, and release step 0c fills it from Google Secret
# Manager before anything is built. This file is the LOCAL fallback: it is what
# your own `npm run dev` and `npm run build` use when nothing else supplies a
# value. Precedence, which is Vite's own:
#
#   1. process.env                     an explicit override; how CI passes a
#                                      repo secret into a preview build
#   2. .env.production.local           the release, from Secret Manager.
#                                      Generated, removed when it finishes, and
#                                      read only by a production build.
#   3. .env.local, .env                this file, filled in by you
#
# `node scripts/client-secrets.mjs --list` prints every variable, which Secret
# Manager secret backs it, and whether a release refuses without it.
#
# An earlier version of this file said these "cannot live in Secret Manager".
# That confused two things. The VALUE does ship inside the bundle every browser
# downloads. That part is true, and it is why only public client identifiers
# belong in a VITE_ variable. But `vite build` reads the value from the machine
# it runs on, and a release can perfectly well put it there. What that buys is
# not secrecy. It is one place to look, and a build that can be reproduced by
# someone who is not holding the original laptop.

# Sentry crash reporting for the React admin at auntie.tribetails.com.
# Secret Manager: ADMIN_WEB_SENTRY_DSN. A release WARNS without it, and today
# every release warns: this has never been set, here or in the store. Web Sentry
# is off for both web apps and that is a known, accepted state, not a
# misconfiguration someone forgot to finish.
#
# The DSN belongs to the `auntieos-admin` project in the `tribetails` Sentry
# org (Settings > Client Keys / DSN), a DIFFERENT project from the portal's, so
# a different DSN. Public by design: it permits write-only event ingestion, and
# what bounds abuse is Sentry-side rate limits and inbound filters.
#
# Leave it empty and reporting is simply off (the app logs
# "Sentry disabled: no VITE_SENTRY_DSN" and runs normally). It has been empty in
# PRODUCTION since 2026-07-23, which is why a two-hour CRITICAL error loop in
# notificationBatchSweep raised no alert at all. That is an argument for turning
# it on, not for a release refusing until someone does: nothing depends on it,
# so the release names it every run and ships.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=

# Tags events with a release so Sentry can group a regression against the deploy
# that introduced it.
#
# DERIVED, not stored: a release sets this to the commit it is shipping. Set it
# locally only if you want your own dev events tagged. A value maintained by
# hand in a .env is a value that names the last release someone remembered to
# edit it for.
VITE_SENTRY_RELEASE=

# reCAPTCHA Enterprise SITE key for admin App Check (#576).
# Secret Manager: ADMIN_WEB_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY. A release REFUSES without it.
#
# NOTE THE TWO NAMES, they are not interchangeable. ADMIN_WEB_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY
# is the SECRET in Secret Manager and the repo secret CI mirrors it with.
# VITE_ADMIN_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY is the BUILD variable the app reads. Creating a
# secret under the VITE_ name would store a value nothing ever fetches.
#
# A site key is a public identifier: it ships in the bundle and says which
# reCAPTCHA key the page is scoring against. The secret half never leaves
# Google. What binds it is the key's allowed-domains list, which is
# auntie.tribetails.com, auntieos-ttpc.web.app and auntieos-ttpc.firebaseapp.com.
# localhost is deliberately off it: local dev uses the debug token instead.
#
# The key was minted and registered with App Check on 2026-08-24. It has its own
# key rather than sharing the portal's `mytribe-appcheck-web`, because O-3 D1
# forbids one key serving two assessment streams. Without a value the admin
# reports App Check `unconfigured` and says so on every boot, which is a real
# state and not a harmless one.
VITE_ADMIN_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY=

# Mapbox public token for the Kin Care route map (#760). It draws the visit
# route over a satellite basemap under the arrival and departure times, with a
# green arrival pin, a red departure pin, a purple marker on the household and a
# blue breadcrumb trail. Operator ruling 2026-09-11, against the previous
# system's visit report.
# Secret Manager: ADMIN_WEB_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN. A release REFUSES without it.
#
# NOTE THE TWO NAMES, as with App Check above. ADMIN_WEB_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN is
# the SECRET and the repo secret CI mirrors it with; VITE_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN is
# the BUILD variable the app reads. Creating a secret under the VITE_ name would
# store a value nothing ever fetches.
#
# THE SAME VALUE AS THE PORTAL IS FINE; THE SAME NAME IS NOT. The portal reads
# PORTAL_WEB_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN, and one URL-restricted `web-maps-public` token
# can legitimately serve both sites. Two secret names is what lets the operator
# rotate or re-restrict one site without editing the other app's build to find
# out what broke, which is the same reason the two Sentry DSNs are two names.
#
# THE URL RESTRICTION IS THE PART THAT BREAKS QUIETLY. The token's allowed
# referrers must include auntie.tribetails.com and the main channel host. A
# valid token whose list omits them still compiles into the bundle, and the
# style request is refused before the first paint: the panel then shows the SVG
# polyline and no error, which looks exactly like having no token at all.
#
# Use the `web-maps-public` token (scopes styles:tiles, styles:read,
# fonts:read), NOT the unrestricted mobile one the Android apps use, and NOT the
# search-scoped token `api/mapbox.ts` proxies through a callable so the browser
# never holds it. Not the server tokens-API secret revoked on 2026-09-03 either:
# that is a different secret for a different job.
#
# A `pk.*` token is a public client credential by design: read-only, and what
# bounds the value of an extracted copy is the referrer restriction plus
# rotation, not secrecy.
#
# Empty locally costs you nothing: RouteMap draws the SVG polyline it always
# has, with no error and no blank panel. Empty in a RELEASE is the reason this
# one refuses: the admin would look entirely healthy while quietly handing the
# office the plainer map, and nobody would think to check.
VITE_MAPBOX_PUBLIC_TOKEN=
